  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>When small amount of carbonate salt is treated with dilute&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;or dilute&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HCl</mi></math>, A colourless, odourless gas&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfenced></math>&nbsp;is evolved with brisk effervescence</p><p>When small amount of the following&nbsp;salts are treated with dilute&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;&nbsp;the following observations are seen.</p><table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" cellspacing=\"1\" style=\"width: 500px;\">\t<tbody>\t\t<tr>\t\t\t<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Carbonate salt&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msubsup><mi>CO</mi><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#8722;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mfenced></math></td>\t\t\t<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Brisk effervescence of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;gas,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>CO</mi><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#8722;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#8594;</mo><msubsup><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#8722;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#8593;</mo></math></td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>\t\t\t<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Sulphide salt&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfenced></math></td>\t\t\t<td style=\"text-align: center;\">A colourless gas&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;with rotten egg smell</td>\t\t</tr>\t</tbody></table><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#8594;</mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>&#8593;</mo></math></p><p>NO<sup>2 &ndash;</sup><sup> </sup>&nbsp;on reaction with dilute H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4&nbsp;</sub>will give brown NO<sub>2</sub>(g) will turn KI solution blue.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>SO</mi><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup></math>on reaction with dilute&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> will give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mfenced></math>, which will turn&nbsp;acidified potassium dichromate solution green.</p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
